      "writtenOn": "2023-06-20T19:48:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There was so much unnecessary code in the old implementation that this is pretty much a complete rewrite and Gerrit\u0027s diff is not super helpful. Where possible, I\u0027ve added comments the new code with codesearch links pointing back to the relevant old implementation.\n\nI have to re-run the numbers, but with the simplification of the edge boundary model, the performance discrepancy from the switch to RP is less drastic than it had been when branching and handling all 9 conditions.",
      "revId": "c87a437d219bc69740688bc7be211f330730bb31",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b0afca6_4bbe7c90",
        "filename": "src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 62,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-20T19:48:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This Map logic had been inlined in various places in the old code, for each of the light `transform` functions, e.g. https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/skia/src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp;drc\u003df24283f9968056c3b8e0a28bd91e615f25a15c8c;l\u003d1044\n\nNotably, the old implementation did not also transform the surfaceScale that applied to the alpha map so you\u0027d get weird effects when zooming in or out where the lights would get farther from the surface and the surface effectively gets flatter.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 62,
        "startChar": 30,
        "endLine": 62,
        "endChar": 33
      },
      "revId": "c87a437d219bc69740688bc7be211f330730bb31",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "23e445d0_a842f083",
        "filename": "src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 81,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-20T19:48:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Instead of having a class hierarchy for CPU lights and a parallel hierarchy for GPU lights, this just tracks the type and union of properties that are used.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 81,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 81,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "c87a437d219bc69740688bc7be211f330730bb31",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2f66206e_20bb71af",
        "filename": "src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 96,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-20T19:48:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The old code for spotlights (https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/skia/src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp;drc\u003df24283f9968056c3b8e0a28bd91e615f25a15c8c;l\u003d1024) had a lot of extra state that was unnecessary.\n\nFirst, it remembered the location and the target, and the normalized direction. In the new shader, we just calculate the direction at construction time and normalize when we upload the layer-space direction to the uniforms. The math works out the same.\n\nIt also had parameters for the inner cutoff and the width of fuzziness for the spotlight. These were all driven by constants and didn\u0027t need to be variables, so they have been moved directly into the shader.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 96,
        "startChar": 39,
        "endLine": 96,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "c87a437d219bc69740688bc7be211f330730bb31",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0e7d2fa3_0ff32a7a",
        "filename": "src/effects/imagefilters/SkLightingImageFilter.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 139,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-20T19:48:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Instead of having two subclasses of image filter for diffuse vs. specular, `Material` just tracks which one it is and stores both k and shininess (unused by the shader if it\u0027s diffuse).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 139,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 139,
        "endChar": 19
      },
      "revId": "c87a437d219bc69740688bc7be211f330730bb31",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
